      Venus   : peak  5880.66 m ; peak*|n^2-nJ^2| = 4.586e+00
      EMB     : peak  7200.33 m ; peak*|n^2-nJ^2| = 2.116e+00
      Mars    : peak 18292.77 m ; peak*|n^2-nJ^2| = 1.492e+00
      Saturn  : peak 286660.46 m ; peak*|n^2-nJ^2| = 5.045e-01
      -> the product is NOT constant (x9 spread): the displacement is dominated by SECULAR pumping (slowly-rotating field torque), not the equilibrium epicycle. Stated plainly; the Mercury number in [8] is therefore a BRACKET, not a law.

[7] RANGING-ACCURACY & CONVENTION PINS (web-verified 2026-06-10)
    Mars range: ~1 m systematics-limited (DSN station calibration), MGS/Odyssey/MRO 1-m class data used in ephemerides — JPL IPN Progress Report 42-190 (Folkner-era, "Station-Specific Errors in Mars Ranging"); EPM2021 fits MRO/ODY ranges with station-bias models (iaaras.ru/en/dept/ephemeris/epm/2021). Adopted sigma = 1.5 m.
    Saturn/Cassini range: normal points ~10 m (Folkner et al. 2008, DE421-era, as quoted in the INPOP19a Cassini analysis: Fienga et al. 2020, A&A 640, A6); DE430-based Cassini residuals show a 12-yr sinusoid of FULL amplitude ~70 m (Hees et al. 2016, AJ 152:94, arXiv:1604.03180 — the Planet Nine ranging paper); INPOP17a shows a ~50 m annual signature (same A&A analysis). Adopted sigma = 25 m (loose variant 75 m).
    Jupiter: DE440 fits Juno radio range + VLBA (Park et al. 2021, AJ 161:105, ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/doc/de440_de441.html). GM_J is measured INDEPENDENTLY by Juno orbital tracking: GM_J = 1.266865341e17 m^3/s^2 with relative uncertainty ~1e-8-1e-9 (Durante et al. 2020, GRL 47, e2019GL086572) — at Juno periapsis |a|~25 m/s^2, F4 is OFF (x~1e11), so the Juno GM_J is F4-clean: any ephemeris GM_J shift >> 1e-8 relative is independently refuted. That is fit config B ("Juno-anchored").
    Mercury/BepiColombo MORE: 2-way range ~1 cm RMS at 4 s integration, in-flight verified (arXiv:2111.04499 INPOP-BepiColombo simulations; arXiv:2211.04881), daily normal points 2026-2028.5 assumed in published simulations.

[8] MERCURY / BEPICOLOMBO SENSITIVITY (order-of-magnitude BRACKET; Mercury not integrated, see [6] caveat)
    Mercury heliocentric response bracket (hostile, PRE-fit): equilibrium-epicycle floor f/n^2 ~ 1.0 m ; Mars-product scaling ~ 293 m
    framework normalization (/33.5): ~3 cm .. 8.7 m PRE-fit; even at the strongest absorption seen in any fit here (7x), the framework signal sits at ~0.43 cm .. 119 cm
    vs BepiColombo MORE sigma ~ 1 cm (in-flight verified): the framework-normalization signal is at-to-above MORE's single-pass noise across the whole bracket — BUT the published MORE campaign window is ~2.5 yr (2026-2028.5), only 0.21 of the 11.86-yr carrier, so much of the slow template is IC/bias-absorbable over so short an arc. Treat as a strong FUTURE check (and a decisive one if MORE-class ranging persists over a decade), not a present bound. Stated as sensitivity, not verdict.

[9] VERDICT (both ways, full weight; decisive numbers = the TRUE NONLINEAR (LM) fits)
  HOSTILE (s = cH_Lambda = 5.418e-10 m/s^2):
    LM fit A (GM_sun, GM_J, 36 IC):      FAIL  (Mars   376.65 m vs 1.5 m ->   251.1x over ; Saturn   968.58 m vs 25.0 m ->   38.7x [vs loose 75.0 m ->  12.9x])
    LM fit B (Juno-anchored GM_J):       FAIL  (Mars   419.41 m ; Saturn   522.20 m)
    LM + kitchen sink (beyond-real):     FAIL  (Mars   314.34 m ->   209.6x over ; Saturn   245.76 m)
  FRAMEWORK (s = a0 = 9.36e-11 m/s^2):
    LM fit A (GM_sun, GM_J, 36 IC):      FAIL  (Mars    12.74 m vs 1.5 m ->     8.5x over ; Saturn    36.89 m vs 25.0 m ->    1.5x [vs loose 75.0 m ->   0.5x])
    LM fit B (Juno-anchored GM_J):       FAIL  (Mars    13.42 m ; Saturn    17.47 m)
    LM + kitchen sink (beyond-real):     FAIL  (Mars     9.32 m ->     6.2x over ; Saturn     7.11 m)
  CRITICAL NORMALIZATION (residual ~ s^2, linearity verified in [6]): instantaneous per-body F4 hides below Mars ranging accuracy only for
    s < 3.21e-11 (conservative fit A) .. 3.76e-11 m/s^2 (kitchen-sink bound) = (0.34 .. 0.40) x a0 = cH_Lambda / (14 .. 17)
    -> BOTH candidate normalizations sit above the survival line: the bath value cH_Lambda by ~17x, the empirical a0 by ~2.9x in s (=8.5x in residual).
  STRUCTURE OF THE KILL: the unabsorbable carrier is the Earth-Jupiter synodic (~1.09 yr) + P_Jup (11.86 yr) response to the Sun's Jupiter-ward anomalous reflex (the r_J^2-anti-correlated template of [2]); at framework normalization the kill is MARS-CARRIED (Cassini alone would be marginal: fit-B Saturn 17.5 m vs 25 m tight / 75 m loose). No realistic nuisance lives at the E-J synodic over a 30-yr arc (annual systematics separate from 1.0921 yr after ~12 yr of data; asteroid templates live at 3-6 yr).
  GM_J NOTE: every fit that may move GM_J wants |dlnGM_J| ~ 2e-7..1e-5 — i.e. 20x..1000x the Juno determination uncertainty (~1e-8, Durante+2020): the absorbed configurations are independently refuted by Juno, which is why the Juno-anchored fit B is the realistic case (and it fails too).

[10] BUG LOG (own-work bugs caught during this run)
    1. Pre-run: stray invalid token (`i in_`) in the name_idx dict comprehension — syntax error, caught on read-through before first execution; fixed.
    2. Pre-run: make_rhs was passed gm=None for non-tuple modes (would crash every F4 run); caught on read-through; fixed to pass GM explicitly.
    3. First full run: numpy matmul on macOS Accelerate raised spurious divide/overflow/invalid RuntimeWarnings inside clean lstsq/projection calls; investigated — solutions verified finite and cross-checked against LAPACK gelsy (max residual difference printed in [4b]); warnings isolated with errstate + isfinite asserts rather than ignored blindly.
    4. First full run: Mercury/BepiColombo extrapolation printed in cm with a misleading "absorption factor from fit A" (9x) — replaced with the kitchen-sink absorption bound and an explicit window-length caveat.
    5. Initial fit-A absorption (8.6x) looked suspiciously WEAK against an analytic prior of stronger absorption — this suspicion was CORRECT in part: see 6-7.
    6. The nonlinear forward check EXPLODED (1e6 m vs 269 m linearized) and frozen-Jacobian Gauss-Newton DIVERGED. Unit test (3-parameter known perturbation, direct integration vs linear prediction) proved the column/parameter mapping exact to ~0.13% of response — so the failure was not a mapping bug: the linearized optimum lives at parameter excursions (e.g. dGM_J/GM_J ~ -8e-6 + large compensating IC moves) whose second-order dynamics break the linear cancellation. A LINEARIZED EMULATION IS NOT VERDICT-GRADE FOR THIS PROBLEM.
    7. Resolution: full Levenberg-Marquardt fits with the Jacobian re-INTEGRATED every iteration (a real mini ephemeris fit), run for fits A/B/kitchen-sink at both normalizations, with a displaced-restart uniqueness probe. The verdict in [9] rests exclusively on these LM numbers; the linear table [4] is retained as a config survey only.

